Problem Statement:

You are given an array prices where prices[i] is the price of a given stock on the $i^{\text{th}}$ day. You want to maximize your profit by choosing a single day to buy one stock and choosing a different day in the future to sell that stock. Return the maximum profit you can achieve from this transaction. If you cannot achieve any profit, return.

Explanation
We iterate through the list of stock prices while tracking the lowest price seen so far (min_price). For each price, we calculate the potential profit if we sold on that day (price - min_price) and update max_profit whenever a higher value is found. This single-pass linear sweep avoids comparing every pair of days.

Complexity Analysis:

Time Complexity: $\mathcal{O}(n)$ — We traverse the prices array of length $n$ exactly once.
Space Complexity: $\mathcal{O}(1)$ — Only two scalar variables (min_price and max_profit) are stored.

Approach NotesTechnique: One-Pass / Greedy Dynamic Programming.
Edge Cases Considered: Continuously declining prices (where profit remains 0), arrays with single/few elements, and large price fluctuations.
Key Insight: You can only sell after you buy; tracking the running minimum price ensures chronological correctness without nested loops.